  {%- comment -%} NF-FOUC-V1: paint the curtain from the first frame.
    #nf-curtain sits after </main>, so the browser streams and paints the whole
    header, announcement bar and hero before it ever reaches that element. That
    is the second of un-decorated store the transition then covers up. This head
    level sheet covers the viewport at first paint and hands off to the real
    curtain, which sits one z-index above it. {%- endcomment -%}
  <style>
    html.nf-preload::before{
      content:""; position:fixed; inset:0; background:#ffffff;
      z-index:99998; pointer-events:none;
    }
    html.nf-preload body{ overflow:hidden !important; }
    /* NF-PERF3-0911: when the 2.5s cap (not DOMContentLoaded) revealed the page, a curtain parsed later must never paint over it */
    html.nf-sheet-capped #nf-curtain:not([style]){ display:none; }
    /* NF-SPEED-0913 (curtain v2): when no curtain is on screen yet the sheet fades out instead of cutting, and a curtain parsed after the page was revealed never shows. */
    html.nf-preload::before{ transition: opacity .25s ease; }
    html.nf-sheet-out::before{ opacity:0; }
    html.nf-lifted #nf-curtain:not([style]){ display:none; }
    /* NF-SPEED-0913 (curtain v2): curtain styles moved here from the end of <body>, so the curtain is styled at first paint. */
    #nf-curtain {
      position: fixed;
      inset: 0;
      background: #ffffff;
      z-index: 99999;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      will-change: transform;
    }
